-- §3  Every classification this tree makes, against the set of classes it declares.
# One row per (relation, class): the relation that classifies, the type its classes belong to,
# what one counted row IS, the class, how many landed in it, and the standing somebody declared
# for it. Every member of every declared set has a row, including the members nothing landed in.
#
# The census moved and the reading stayed
#   The eight arms that drive each enum's members from `enum_range` are
#   `epistemics/class_cells.sqlc` now, because a law and two reports wanted them too and a fold
#   written twice is one map with two unnamed copies. What is left here is the reading: the same
#   rows, in the order each type declares its members, which is the `CASE` precedence of the
#   relation that assigns them.
#
# `subject` is why two numbers in this column are not comparable
#   The eight relations count in six units, so `balls` compares down a relation's own arm and
#   nowhere else: 303 computations and 32 rules are not 335 of anything.
#   `epistemics/class_sets.sqlc` declares the unit and §3b shows what happens to it under a scope.
#
# An empty class is a referral, and it now has an address
#   The classes nothing lands in are still printed, and this program still asserts nothing about
#   them. `algebra/class_domain.sqlc` is what asserts: every class of every declared codomain has
#   exactly one declared standing, and that standing agrees with the count in both directions. So
#   a zero a reader sees below stands beside the reason it is a zero, and a zero with no reason
#   fails the build rather than being observed here.
-- epistemics/classes.sqlc beside its declared standings, in each type's declared order.
SELECT c.relation AS "relation!", c.codomain AS "codomain!", c.class AS "class!",
       c.subject AS "subject?", c.balls AS "balls!",
       d.standing::text AS "standing?", d.reason AS "reason?"
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/classes.sqlc)
) c
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(epistemics/class_domain.sqlc)
) d ON d.relation = c.relation AND d.class = c.class
ORDER BY c.relation, c.ord
